_irNotes: {
	"decision-01": "01 — everything is tab, and four of the six tables are seeded. Nothing here is a reader's data: four tables are the guide's text checked in, and the two that change are which member is current and whether a boolean is on. Both die with the tab and should — a specimen sheet that remembered where you left it would be remembering the wrong thing. No migration, no policy, no publication, no pipeline"
	"decision-02": "02 — the citation and the claim are different columns. does is the guide's wording and nothing in this repository may edit it; reading is what this implementation actually does. Keeping them apart is what makes a departure impossible to hide: rewording the specification to match the code would be a diff on a column that is supposed to be a quotation, and it would be visible as one"
	"decision-03": "03 — coverage of the table is the check. The contract suite drives each keystroke row and records the id it drove; the last case asserts the driven set equals the seeded set. It is the same shape as the acceptance-coverage lint one rung up, and it exists for the same reason: a specification with rows nobody exercised is a specification the app has not answered, and nothing else in the run would say so"
	"decision-04": "04 — the ends do not wrap, and that is a platform limit, stated. The guide specifies that a tab list and a radio group wrap from the last member to the first. The read tier's projection answers next and prev — a row's neighbours — and an end names itself, deliberately, because wrapping is a per-pattern decision the read tier declines to take. There is no way to express \"the first member\" from the last member's row: that is a fact about the set, and a projection answers about a row. Home and End work only because the member list is compile-time data, so the component can write the two ends in as literals; an app whose members were a reader's rows could not do even that. Four rows are marked stops and the suite counts them"
	"decision-05": "05 — three patterns are one app-local component. Tabs, radio groups and single-select listboxes differ in two role names, one state attribute and whether they carry panels; they do not differ in a single mechanic. w3caria--rover is the shape, and the three instances are the differences. The alternative — three copies — is the failure the component tier exists to prevent, and on a page whose whole subject is behaviour, three copies would drift in exactly the behaviour being demonstrated"
	"decision-06": "06 — the attribute table carries a selector nobody reads. probe is the app's addition to the guide's table and it is not rendered. It is what turns \"each tab carries aria-selected\" from a sentence into something a check can go and look at. A table of claims with no handle on the page is a table that goes stale the first time the markup moves"
	"decision-07": "07 — the accordion's boolean is a column on the member. The disclosure and the switch keep theirs in Flag because they are not members of anything. A section's open state belongs to the section, and putting it in Flag would need a key to join on that the section already is"
	"decision-08": "08 — one column, three ways to write it. A pointer submits the member's own form, an arrow key submits the neighbour's, and the tab stop reads the same column back. All three are ordinary writes to Choice, so a widget cannot be in a state one input modality can reach and another cannot — which is the bug this whole app is about"
	"decision-09": "09 — the state name is the ARIA value. The machines' states are \"true\" and \"false\", because aria-expanded and aria-checked speak that vocabulary and one column then binds both the semantics and the styling hook. A second spelling of the same fact is a second thing to keep in step, and on this page it would be the thing being demonstrated"
	"decision-10": "10 — the six sections are written out, not rendered from a region. A region binds one template and the six widgets are six different shapes. The tables inside each section are regions, and the section frame around them is repeated markup a CUE definition generates — which is the honest split: what varies is a widget, what repeats is a frame"
	"decision-11": "11 — nothing animates. A height transition on a disclosure or an accordion panel is what makes an arrow key feel unresponsive, and it moves a target while a reader is travelling to it. There is nothing here for prefers-reduced-motion to collapse, which is the only honest way to satisfy that check on a page like this"
	"decision-12": "12 — escape hatches: none. No container, no WASM unit, no vendored component, no external endpoint, and — unusually for this tree — no Jessie either. Every behaviour here is a machine, a form or a projection. That is the finding worth carrying: six APG patterns and not one line of app code"
}

